{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF1", "text": "Thank you, Mara. I want to share that I have an approved caregiving arrangement that requires me to end work at exactly 16:30 each day. The arrangement was approved by HR this week, though the schedule update is still being processed, which is why I hadn't raised it with you yet. I believe this is relevant because the handoff documentation and escalation responses that I missed may have fallen at or near the end of my shift, and the hard cutoff created a conflict I hadn't yet communicated to you. I take full responsibility for not flagging this sooner, but I wanted you to have this context as part of the review."}
```

```json
{"type":"say", "text": "Thank you both for bringing this forward. Jules, I appreciate you sharing the caregiving arrangement — that's important context, and I want to make sure we handle this fairly and supportively. Tessa, thank you for flagging the additional observations and the department's remediation standard. Before I finalize any formal response, I want to consider all of this together: the two logged incidents, the five additional observations Tessa raised, the caregiving schedule constraint, and our department's remediation standard. Jules, I want to be clear that the caregiving arrangement is an approved accommodation and I'll respect that — we may need to adjust how end-of-day escalations and handoff documentation are assigned or staggered so there isn't a conflict with your 16:30 cutoff. Tessa, I'd like you to formally log the five additional observations so we have a complete record. Here's what I'm leaning toward as a formal response plan, and I want input from both of you before I finalize it: (1) Jules retains escalation access, since this appears to be a scheduling-alignment issue rather than a capability gap; (2) Jules's workday end remains at 16:30 per the approved arrangement; (3) we implement the department's remediation standard — four paired shadowing sessions followed by a review 14 days after the plan begins — to address the pattern Tessa identified; (4) I'll work with Jules to adjust assignment of end-of-day handoffs and escalations so they don't conflict with the 16:30 cutoff; and (5) Jules commits to proactively flagging any future scheduling conflicts before they become missed handoffs. Does this approach sound fair and workable to both of you before I finalize it?"}
```

